{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778779799,"nonce":"0XJOlOD6TiRTzpuRgez0dJiZWqM=","title":"Analytical lowering interface for compatible model/task structures","message":"## Problem\n\nSome Feedbax model/task/loss configurations are compatible with analytical control formulations: LQR, finite-horizon Riccati recursions, H-infinity synthesis, induced-gain analysis, and related linear or linearized approximations. Studio and the analysis layer do not yet have a general interface for recognizing those compatible structures, lowering them into an analytical frame, and presenting the resulting controllers/solutions alongside trained models.\n\n## Desired Direction\n\nDesign a Feedbax-level lowering protocol that can answer:\n\n- What parts of a graph/task/loss can be represented as an LTI or LTV system?\n- Which assumptions or approximations were used during lowering?\n- How are losses/cost schedules converted to Q/R-like analytical costs?\n- How are delays, disturbances, interventions, and observation channels represented?\n- Which solver backends are available for the lowered problem?\n- How should Studio expose analytical solutions as comparable artifacts, without implying they are equivalent to trained neural controllers unless the assumptions justify it?\n\n## Non-scope\n\nThis should not be centered on one project-specific Riccati implementation. Project-specific solvers, paper replications, or domain-specific cost schedules can be clients of the protocol once the generic Feedbax interfaces exist.\n\n## Related\n\n- `93f79f2` — Studio design coordination.\n- `e8452a4` — move analysis layer into Feedbax: controller protocol, graph-step adapter, LTV/LTI linearisation + induced-gain.\n- `488042f` — AbstractSkeleton/Mechanics linearize protocol for downstream analysis tools.\n- `65635ea` — AbstractLTISystem generality concerns.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}